    fields:  # 21aa
      description: "FOUND 2026-08-31 while classifying the subprocess catalogue for INV-lozat. That\nitem shipped ONE of the three categories ADR-0049 ruling 1 divides the family\ninto; this is the second, filed rather than folded in silently.\n\nTHE CLASSIFICATION, over all 116 subprocess symbols in the 14 shipped\ncatalogues, by ruling 1 (\"does this call return a value whose content the far\nside chose?\"):\n\n  (A) RETURNS THE CHILD'S CONTENT -- 18 symbols, 8 languages. SHIPPED by\n      INV-lozat as ipc_recv + simultaneous.\n  (B) RETURNS A HANDLE the child fills later -- THIS ITEM.\n  (C) RETURNS AN EXIT STATUS OR UNIT -- correctly launch-only, unchanged.\n\nCATEGORY B, enumerated:\n\n  c           stdlib::popen                    -> FILE*\n  python      subprocess::Popen                -> Popen object (.stdout)\n              os::popen                        -> file object\n  go          os/exec::Command / CommandContext-> *Cmd (StdoutPipe)\n  java        java.lang.ProcessBuilder::start  -> Process (getInputStream)\n              java.lang.Runtime::exec          -> Process\n  rust        std::process::Command::spawn     -> Child (already has\n                                                  ChildStdout::read rowed\n                                                  ipc_recv -- see below)\n  haskell     System.Process::createProcess    -> (stdin,stdout,stderr,handle)\n              System.Process.Typed::startProcess\n  javascript  child_process::spawn / fork      -> ChildProcess\n              child_process::exec / execFile   -> ChildProcess, stdout via cb\n  objc        NSTask::launch                   -> output via NSPipe\n  swift       Process (Foundation)             -> output via Pipe\n  elixir      Port::open                       -> port, data arrives as msgs\n  erlang      erlang::open_port                -> same\n  scala       Process::run / ProcessBuilder::run -> Process handle\n\nTHIS IS THE `net_listen` SHAPE ONE CHANNEL OVER. ADR-0049 ruling 1 answers NO\nfor every row above: the call hands back a handle, and the crossing happens\nlater, in a scope the call does not name. Ruling 2 then says a deferred crossing\nis DISCLOSED and never minted, and that it must SHADOW the corresponding data\nboundary so a disclosure tag does not become a false-all-clear generator under\nINV-buzab. `net_listen` shadows `net_recv`; the analogue here would shadow\n`ipc_recv`.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT OBVIOUSLY WORTH DOING, and why the item says so up front. The\nlaunch rows ALREADY carry `subprocess`, which is in OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES, so every\none of these calls already withholds a clean verdict on EVERY boundary\n(`declares_opaque_crossing`). A deferred-crossing tag would be strictly weaker\nthan the opacity these rows already assert. The question this item exists to\nanswer is therefore NOT \"should these mint?\" (ruling 1 says no, settled) but\n\"does the corpus contain a real crossing these rows leave unrepresented, that\nsome OTHER row could carry?\" -- ruling 3's finding-level test.\n\nWHERE THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS, and it is not symmetric across languages. rust\nalready rows the far end: `ChildStdout::read` / `ChildStderr::read` / \n`Child::wait_with_output` are ipc_recv, so a rust `spawn` has its crossing\nrepresented one call later and needs nothing. python has NO row for\n`Popen.stdout.read` or `.communicate()`; java has none for\n`Process.getInputStream`; go has none for `Cmd.StdoutPipe`. So the likely\ndeliverable is not a deferred-crossing tag at all -- it is the MISSING FAR-END\nROWS in the four languages where rust has them and nobody else does, which is\nthe same intra-tree inconsistency INV-lozat found one call earlier.\n\nBEFORE BUILDING: measure emission shape first. INV-lozat did not, and shipped a\nchange whose rows classify only 23 of 217 candidate call sites on beads (10.6%)\nbecause Go leaves a `cmd := exec.Command(...)` receiver untyped. `Popen.stdout`\nis an ATTRIBUTE on a bare local and will be worse. Publish the number before\nchoosing a design."  # 21aa
- op: discuss  # 21de
  at: '2026-09-02T05:55:20Z'  # 21de
  by: agent  # 21de
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 21de
  clock: 2  # 21de
  nonce: 21de  # 21de
  message: "MEASURED 2026-09-02 on dev 28998e145c. This item's stated precondition --\n\"BEFORE BUILDING: measure emission shape first ... Publish the number before\nchoosing a design\" -- is discharged, and THE NUMBER REFUTES THE DELIVERABLE\nTHIS ITEM GUESSED AT.\n\n*** THE MISSING FAR-END ROWS WOULD BE INERT. *** This item's closing paragraph\nsays \"the likely deliverable is not a deferred-crossing tag at all -- it is the\nMISSING FAR-END ROWS in the four languages where rust has them and nobody else\ndoes\". Adding those rows changes nothing.\n\nMETHOD. Purpose-built probes in python/java/go/c through the shipped analyzers,\nthen the emitted dst put through the PRODUCTION classifier\n(classify_call_in_catalog) with the candidate row added to a live catalogue\ncopy. Two arms per case: the module slot the analyzer ACTUALLY emits, and the\nslot it would emit if the receiver's type resolved.\n\nWHAT THE ANALYZERS EMIT for the category-B far end:\n\n    p = subprocess.Popen([...], stdout=PIPE)\n    out, err = p.communicate()   -> python:external:0-0:communicate:external_symbol\n    data = p.stdout.read()       -> python:external:0-0:read:external_symbol\n    Process p = new ProcessBuilder(\"ls\").start()\n    p.getInputStream()           -> java:external:0-0:getInputStream:external_symbol\n    cmd := exec.Command(\"ls\")\n    cmd.StdoutPipe()             -> go:external:0-0:StdoutPipe:external_symbol\n\nEvery one lands on the `external` SENTINEL in the module slot. The LAUNCH\nresolves in the same file and the same function (python emits\n`instantiates -> python:subprocess:0-0:Popen`), so the binding is visible in\nthe graph; the far-end call is where the type is dropped.\n\nTHE DIFFERENTIAL, same candidate row each time, module slot the only variable:\n\n    python  row module=subprocess.Popen  name=communicate\n        slot external          -> None        <-- what is emitted\n        slot subprocess.Popen  -> ipc_recv\n        slot Popen             -> ipc_recv\n        slot subprocess        -> ipc_recv\n    java    row module=java.lang.Process name=getInputStream\n        slot external          -> None        <-- what is emitted\n        slot java.lang.Process -> ipc_recv\n        slot Process           -> ipc_recv\n    go      row module=os/exec.Cmd       name=StdoutPipe\n        slot external          -> None        <-- what is emitted\n        slot os/exec.Cmd       -> ipc_recv\n        slot Cmd               -> ipc_recv\n        slot os/exec           -> ipc_recv\n\nSo THE ROWS ARE PERFECTLY MATCHABLE, at the exact module path, at the bare type\nname, and at the package. Not one of them is reached by what the producer\nemits. The blocker is RECEIVER TYPING, not the catalogue -- which is the same\ndiagnosis INV-lozat's 10.6% (23 of 217 beads sites) was already pointing at,\nand this item quoted that number while still guessing the rows were the\ndeliverable.\n\nMY FIRST RUN OF THIS PROBE WAS WRONG AND THE CONTROL IS WHAT CAUGHT IT.\nAppending to `catalog.primitives` does not update the lookup indices --\n`_rebuild_indices()` must be called -- so the first pass reported None for\nEVERY arm including the resolved ones, which reads as \"the rows are\nunmatchable\" and would have been a much stronger and completely false claim. A\npositive control on rows that already exist (python subprocess.run ->\nsubprocess, java ProcessBuilder.start -> subprocess) is what exposed it. Kept\nin the run rather than checked once and dropped.\n\nPOPULATION, counted separately per language because one cohort cannot answer\nfor all four. COUNTING RULE: occurrences in non-test source, receiver NOT\nverified to come from a launch, so these bound the population from ABOVE.\n\n  python  .communicate()          11   over 1,826 .py files\n          .stdout.read/readline()  0\n  go      .StdoutPipe/.StderrPipe() 5  over 22,749 .go files\n  c       popen()                   0  over 134 .c files (thin, not a strong zero)\n  java    .getInputStream()       141  over 10 java repositories\n\n*** THE JAVA FIGURE NEEDS THE SECOND CUT AND THE FIRST COHORT WAS A DEAD\nCONTROL. *** My first census ran over beads/cilium/jaeger/caddy/gocryptfs/\npretix/kserve/cert-manager/alertmanager/prometheus and reported java=0. That\ncohort contains ZERO .java files. A zero from a cohort with no java in it is\nnot a result, and I nearly published it as one -- this item's own family\n(a dead control reads as confirmation) caught one level down.\n\nOn a real java cohort (gradle, keycloak, jenkins, netty, dagger, eclipse.jdt.ls,\ncommons-compress, jna, jedis, rocksdb) the 141 sites are dominated by receivers\nthat are NOT a Process: java.net.Socket.getInputStream is ALREADY rowed\nnet_recv, and URLConnection is the other common receiver. Files containing\ngetInputStream that ALSO launch a process: 21 of 125 (~17%), so the Process\nfar end is roughly 24 sites, not 141.\n\nTOTAL, honestly bounded: ~40 category-B far-end sites over 18 repositories, and\nNONE of them reachable by any row today.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM'S ACTUAL QUESTION, which was \"does the corpus contain\na real crossing these rows leave unrepresented, that some OTHER row could\ncarry?\" The crossing IS unrepresented, and NO row can carry it today. Ruling 3\nis therefore not the binding constraint; receiver typing is. Re-scoped: the\ndeliverable is not rows, it is resolving the receiver from its local binding --\nthe mechanism WI-lipis already shipped for go's `bufio.NewScanner(f)` (read the\nlast binding of a bare local in the enclosing function and stamp from it), and\nthe same family as WI-dizag (rust struct-field receiver) and INV-linub L3.\nUntil that lands, ANY row added here is inert, and adding it would create the\nappearance of coverage with none.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR INV-muhij, whose census closed with \"the question that replaced\n[step 2]: can the ordering test anchor on the EFFECT call rather than the\nconstructor -- which is WI-kanor's family (handle-returning launches) seen from\nthe taint side.\" ANSWERED: NOT TODAY. The effect call does not resolve to\nanything the catalogue names, so there is no effect-call row to anchor an\nordering test on. INV-muhij's remedy (3) cannot be built on that anchor and\nneeds a different one.\n\nNOT CHANGING PRIORITY. The population (~40) and the inertness both argue for a\nlower rank, but this item was already re-ranked once and priority changes on\nthis tracker have gone through the owner. Proposed, not applied."  # 21de
